Z||000022||Sootah||11-28-2001||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by yo_sweet:<BR><B>Mine is, wont do anything for me , can't please him , refers to my bought and paid for fiero as his, freaks out when i do custom work to it, gets mad when people try to help me out<P>can't win , i pretty much think i hate him...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lightweight. If you want to hear about trouble w/ your father I will be happy to share my many, many experiences with mine. Will have to start a new topic though. At least yours hasn't kicked you out and essentially turned his back on you.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">        <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>The Black Beauty</A> (85 GT) - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>Bustedato</A> (86 SE, Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter<BR><B>MSN IM</B>: I_R_Sootah@hotmail.com<BR></sup>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula88||11-29-2001||12:14 AM||||My father is the greatest man I've known in my life.  We butted heads when I was a teenager (you always do), but fortunately we came to respect each other as individuals later on.  <P>My dad is 75 now, and in a nursing home with Alzheimer's.  He still recognizes me from time to time.  When I was growing up, he lamented not being around as much as he wanted to becaue of work.  As I grew up and moved out, I made the same excuses.  Trust me on this.  You can't get back lost time.  Don't waste what you have.  My dad & I had some really great memories.  I still have them.  He doesn't.  But, the one time he saw my Fiero, he liked it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  He said it reminded him of his '61 Corvair that he taught me how to drive in.<P>Take a moment to thank him for his concern for you and ask him to understand you.  He just may try.  <P>One of the biggest eye opening discussions I had as a teenager was with my grandmother.  She told me I was very much like my father was when he was my age.  He remembered how he was like at my age, and it scared the sh!t out of him!  It really helped me to understand his point of view.  ||||reg||3
Z||000024||artherd||11-29-2001||02:11 AM||||The Living Years<P><BR>Every generation<BR>Blames the one before<BR>And all of their frustrations<BR>Come beating on your door<P>I know that I'm a prisoner<BR>To all my Father held so dear<BR>I know that I'm a hostage<BR>To all his hopes and fears<BR>I just wish I could have told him in the living years<P>Crumpled bits of paper<BR>Filled with imperfect thought<BR>Stilted conversations<BR>I'm afraid that's all we've got<P>You say you just don't see it<BR>He says it's perfect sense<BR>You just can't get agreement<BR>In this present tense<BR>We all talk a different language<BR>Talking in defence<P>Say it loud, say it clear<BR>You can listen as well as you hear<BR>It's too late when we die<BR>To admit we don't see eye to eye<P>So we open up a quarrel<BR>Between the present and the past<BR>We only sacrifice the future<BR>It's the bitterness that lasts<P>So Don't yield to the fortunes<BR>You sometimes see as fate<BR>It may have a new perspective<BR>On a different day<BR>And if you don't give up, and don't give in<BR>You may just be O.K.<P>Say it loud, say it clear<BR>You can listen as well as you hear<BR>It's too late when we die<BR>To admit we don't see eye to eye<P>I wasn't there that morning<BR>When my Father passed away<BR>I didn't get to tell him<BR>All the things I had to say<P>I think I caught his spirit<BR>Later that same year<BR>I'm sure I heard his echo<BR>In my baby's new born tears<BR>I just wish I could have told him in the living years<P>Say it loud, say it clear<BR>You can listen as well as you hear<BR>It's too late when we die<BR>To admit we don't see eye to eye<P><BR>------------------<P>My Parents both are the most wonderful people you will meet.  I have them to thank for the man I am today, and as every day goes by I grow to appricate more and more what they did for me during my 'formative' years.<P>I'm fortunate enough to realize all this at the age of 21, with both parents still allive and (nominally, for now) healthy.<P>Sieze the moment, there are no guarentees for tommorow!  Life is too, too terribly short, for anything but love.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000025||GT Bastard||11-29-2001||05:13 AM||||My dad and I never talked much when I lived at home.  I mean we never REALLY talked.  I'm 22 and I already regret not giving him more respect.  It's human nature to take things for granted though.  If he were to die I would be seriously crushed. I know he went through a lot of hard work to provide for us and I never thanked him for it.  Aside from that he's one of the nicest people I know.   ||||reg||1
Z||000026||84fierotrevor||11-29-2001||06:10 AM||||my dad is a @#$%^ son of a @#$%<BR>If i ever saw him on the street I would beat the ****  outa him ! if he hadn't had that gun when he ****ing kidnapped me he would be dead right now.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Indy-2M4||11-29-2001||07:09 AM||||Artherd, that really says it all. I am very close to both of my parents and it kills me to see them getting older, poor health, etc. There comes a time when (most of us anyway) we think back to all the times they were there for us......well, I'm gonna be there for mine. They helped me be the person I am today and I thank them. I love you Mom & Dad...<P>YoSweet....just give it some time....<P>Kathy||||reg||1
Z||000028||SteveJ||11-29-2001||08:41 AM||||My father was a chain smoking alcoholic who was financially irresponsible. I knew I could do better with my life and my children, so I did.||||reg||1
Z||000029||thomas_l||11-29-2001||09:09 AM||||The relationship with my folks was the best thing I got back when I dumped my ex six years ago. Now I am watching them get old and thinking what a sh1thead I was for allowing my ex to push us apart for eight good years before we split up. Once they're gone there's no more chances. I see my current wife everytime her Mom's birthday and day she passed get really agitated and sad. The closest she can get now is a memorial bench on the beach in Santa Barbara on the occaision we take a trip out there. The bench doesn't say much. And I think how lucky I am that I can pick up the phone and call mine.<P>Hopefully one day you will mature enough to look past the small stuff. I didn't until I was 30.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Pheagey||11-29-2001||09:19 AM||||   Yes, my father is an *******  too. True he took care of me but only on the most minamal level. Basicly, he payd the bills-after that he ignored me. ******* .<BR>   Im 20 now, on my own and talked to hi once in 2 years for about 5 mins. only 'cause I needed info to get into college. <BR>   I dont wish anything bad on him, just..I dunno.....<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed;<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>"The car formaly know as, and has now reverted back to being known as, Pheagey's Fiero."||||reg||8
Z||000031||yo_sweet||11-29-2001||09:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84fierotrevor:<BR><B>my dad is a @#$%^ son of a @#$%<BR>If i ever saw him on the street I would beat the ****  outa him ! if he hadn't had that gun when he ****ing kidnapped me he would be dead right now.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>holla sh!t!!!!!<P><P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
Z||000032||84Bill||11-29-2001||10:39 AM||||I saved my parents house from sheriff sale a few years back. paid 3400 to stop the sale and 1200 every 3 months to pay the arrears for 3 years while they lived in Florida. They didnt care about the place at all and were willing to let it go UNTIL I nearly had it out of hock. Suddenly my dad decides to sell it, I figure no problem I'll get a decent cut. YEAH. All I got was 950 bucks of the 69,000 the house went for. <P>My dad had a junk 85 Dodge K car, I needed a car so I gave him 50 bucks for it. The 2.5 engine ran like crap so I did some investigation and found a burnt valve. Got the head fixed up for 300 and ran it for a week before the cam snaped in two so another 150 and I had a good running car. For six months I drove the wheels off it until I came across my Fiero. To make a long story short my dad wiped his car so I loaned him my dodge that I was going to sell for 1300. 6 months later I left Florida and told my dad to sell my car which he was still driving and another six months later he does and kept the money. To this day I still have not seen a penny from the sale of that car. <BR>My dad is a dork headed back stabbing snit head Azz hole||||reg||1
Z||000033||Formula88||11-29-2001||10:54 AM||||Parent's are just like everyone else.  Some are great, some just get by, and some are a waste of air.  I count myself very lucky that I've had two great parents, even with the problems I had growing up.  And I was fortunate enough to tell them both this while it still mattered.<P>My brother in law hated his father so much, he even changed his name because he wanted to have nothing to do with that man.  If there are problems of that magnitude, I'm sorry, but I respect you doing what you have to do to move on and take care of yourself.  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||84Bill||11-29-2001||12:47 PM||||Oh I almost forgot<BR>I still call them once or twice a year just to say hi. I don't bring up any of the aformentiond but it definatly put a damper on my "closeness" with them. It's hard to burry the anger especially when a simple admission of wrong and apology are all I want.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Simple Fiero||11-29-2001||01:55 PM||||My dad according to the ppl around me is a easy going cool guy.<BR>Then it comes to me. See the thing is we work together and he is my boss so maybe that put a strain on our relationship. Or maybe it was the fact that he cheated and cheated on my mother when i was a youngster.<BR>They are divorced now and although I can never seem to pls my mother I love her way more then him.<BR>He is the kinda guy who, if I was dying on the floor in front of him, he would just look at me and tell me how much character I am building.<BR>But I do have to say this about him, he never beat me or abused me in any way. And that I appreciate about him.<BR>But I would just love for him to live in my shoes for one day and see what it is like from the other side, i bet you he would not like it very much.<BR>Now my grandfather on the other hand RULES!! He lets my father and his brothers and me do what ever we want, we can go over use his garage anytime and mess it up and he doesn't care, not that he would want us to put anything away.<BR>So Yo Sweet I think some of us on here know what you are going through. You just have to tough it out and hope that you never have a mean old *****  of a step mother, now there are a few stories i can tell you there about my youth!<BR>Or maybe you just need a cool wicked awesome grandfather to take his place <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000036||Cyberkhat||11-29-2001||04:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lady Pegasus:<BR><B>I would rather have an *******  for a dad then no dad at all.  I was 15 when my father was diagnosed with lung cancer, only it wasn't that simple </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My Dad treated me like sh*t or so I thought when I was a teenager.He died when I was 15 also. Now I realize how good he really was to me,just trying to steer me in the right direction. God I miss him.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||REDHOTT88||11-29-2001||07:12 PM||||<BR>Just testing...<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B>V6 5 speed<BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red & shiny all over - with all the options!)</FONT><BR><B>1986 2m6</B> 4 speed<BR>( Silver daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000038||Mach10||11-29-2001||07:14 PM||||I'm seeing a LOT of the teen-angst thing... Grandfather rules because he lets you walk all over him? Appreciate what you got. It ain't so bad. <P>There ARE some truly nasty people out there. So as long as he doesn't get home at 3am drunk as a lord, and starts beating you and your mother until 9am... Don't complain. <P>On the other hand, have you tried TALKING to the guy, instead of just harboring resentment? Yeah? Well try again. The key is TIMING. Didn't get along terribly well with my father until I was 20... Since then it's been cool...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000039||Patrick's Dad||11-29-2001||11:17 PM||||I seem to be somewhere in the middle here....<P>Dad and I weren't that close when he left, although he didn't leave town. It seemed that he fulfilled the duties of the divorce decree, and not much else. He wanted to be my "buddy," and talk about my sexual conquests (whether real or imagined). Things warmed a little bit, until he shacked up with a shrew for several years. He finally left, and things seemed to warm a bit again. He has a new girlfriend (17 years his junior, although at 67, I guess that isn't *too* bad) who is a lot nicer than his past....<P>He's moved in with her, and is, essentially, there with her family (Her mother, her son,  daughters and grandchildren), about an hour+ drive time. Now (well, a few weeks ago) I get an E-Mail stating that they have set a date to get married; next August. I'll have three stepsisters and a stepbrother. This wierds me out a bit.<P>BTW, "Living Years" and "Cat's in the Cradle" both tug at me. My boy isn't going to have to worry about these things. My girl (both; wife and daughter) won't, either.||||reg||1
Z||000040||fierospeeder||11-30-2001||12:40 AM||||Im 22 and a caraholic.<P>Im going to school and living at home.  My mom and i have a really good relationship and she buys me, if she has money, whatever i want.  But I try not to ask for anything cause i have a job, and she doesn't make that much money for me to get a brand new mustang.  &lt;joke&gt;  She does get my sis anything.   My mom is the greatest, but i dont really talk to her with my problems.  Im like tim allen on the tv show, im a guy, dont express feelings.  But we do share interests, and she does allow me to keep my car hobby, but does get pissed off that i got 3 cars.  And a 4th project car just today, vw beetle.  <P>My dad and i dont really talk.  Its like a coworker relationship, but a coworker you dont really hang out with.  He does yell at me for things i do.  Or isn't happy for me if i bought more tools or got something for my car.   "now theres more crap in the house", eventhough my dad saves every little thing that my mom and i toss out.  My dad isn't really handy around the house and i do 75% of the household work if my mom asks 10 times.  Hes more into watching tv and recycling newspapers.  Hes more into sports where im more into car things.  My dad hasn't really given me anything except money on xmas and my bday.  He does buy us food and so does my mom.  If my dad passed away, i wouldn't know how i would feel.  I know if my mom did, i would be heartbroken, cause we have always been close.<P>I can say there are people in worse situations and im just telling how it is in my house.  I can say, i am lucky to be growing up as i did.  Just to have the things i have, compared to those that dont have anything at all.  I use to think how it would be if my parents were total yuppies etc... and my way of life would be very restricted.  <P><BR>68 vw beetle body<BR>70 vw chassis<P>------------------<BR>Fiero Technician<P>74 Vw Beetle<BR>83 Honda Nighthawk 550<BR>84 Pontiac Fiero<BR>87 Ford Thunderbird Turbo<P>Selling a car is like giving your own kid for adoption.||||reg||1
Z||000041||yo_sweet||11-30-2001||09:41 AM||||My dads been actually kinda cool lately after my mom told him i hated his guts and wanted him to die and burn in hell!. At the time i meant it , not so sure anymore..<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
